WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.040
- By the end of the year,

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:02.150
I had a spreadsheet

00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:05.380
of every topic that most black comics

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:08.130
nationally were discussing
and I just went, okay, cool.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:11.743
- You've got your perspective.

00:00:15.321 --> 00:00:16.154
I just want to be happy.

00:00:16.154 --> 00:00:17.490
Don't you want to be happy?

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:23.860
So let's get right into
what I think you do

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:25.110
as well as anybody in the world,

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:27.040
which is why we're so
happy to have you here.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:28.900
From a content creation standpoint,

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:30.990
from a creative standpoint,

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:34.210
how do you personally, as a
human, like, think about that?

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:35.790
How did you come,

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:38.980
what kind of process even at a child,

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:40.400
back to being four,

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.680
did you have comedy around the house?

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:44.090
Did it come natural?

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:46.650
Were you distracting
teachers in third grade?

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:48.630
Like what was your early process

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:51.670
of what has become your
professional process?

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:53.190
- Comedy was escapism for me.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:54.910
I wasn't in the same school system

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:56.880
for more than two years until high school.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.147
So it was the constant reacclimation

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.070
to the social scenes and circles

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:06.300
of people around Birmingham.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.870
And like, I would take the radio,

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:11.630
my father was in journalism,

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:14.240
and so I would follow him
to speaking engagements

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:15.090
and stuff like that.

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:18.644
And he was a lot more wokedy woke than me

00:01:18.644 --> 00:01:20.667
in terms of his content
and what he spoke for

00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:21.500
and what he fought for,

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.773
but the oration part of it
I'm sure I got from him.

00:01:26.317 --> 00:01:30.800
I'd say the first big deal
was in the sixth grade.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:32.240
I decided to make my own comic book.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.440
I was obsessed with comic strips.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.670
We read the newspaper as a
family every Sunday morning.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:37.503
That was our thing.

00:01:37.503 --> 00:01:40.280
We'd sit the paper down and
pull it apart like a turkey

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.210
and everybody would read
a different section.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:46.310
And so I was into Marmaduke and Garfield,

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:48.590
Calvin and Hobbes, the whole run.

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:52.680
And I drew my own comic book

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:56.630
and then I would charge
students 50 cents to read it.

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:57.690
And then you had to bring it back.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:00.310
It was like a one comic book blockbuster.

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:03.490
And I think that was my first hustle.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:07.370
The concept of a copy machine
and a stapler just didn't.

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:08.520
- You missed it.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:09.353
You missed it.

00:02:09.353 --> 00:02:11.950
- I said, no, this is the only one,

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:13.860
you can have to give it back on Thursday

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:16.100
because I'm getting ice
cream money from Terrance.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:19.001
- Roy, on the record,

00:02:19.001 --> 00:02:21.800
who tried to not bring it back on time

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:25.526
in the history of this business, anybody?

00:02:25.526 --> 00:02:27.540
- There was a guy Trey McClure,

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:28.373
we're friends now,

00:02:28.373 --> 00:02:30.120
so I can call him out by his full name.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.820
He held on to Mr. Goose a
little longer than he should,

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.010
didn't pay any late fees.

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:35.150
It's all love, Trey.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:36.830
I know this is gonna get back to you

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:38.423
'cause Alabama likes to gossip.

00:02:39.268 --> 00:02:41.480
So for me, that was kind of where,

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:42.970
you know, being funny started

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:46.880
and high school was really
where things started moving.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:48.560
I got cut from my high
school baseball team

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:49.480
my freshman year,

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:53.450
but I wanted to learn
and see, well, if I suck,

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:54.790
what are they doing right?

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.380
So I volunteered to be the
bat boy, equipment manager,

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:58.810
whatever you want to call it,

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:01.050
and I just trailed the varsity kids around

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:03.160
for a whole season.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:04.850
And they did the food fundraisers

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:05.840
where you sell the chips

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:07.860
to raise money to pay for the uniform

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:10.490
and I outsold everyone in the school.

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:11.323
- [Gary] Interesting.

00:03:11.323 --> 00:03:13.560
- And so that was,

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.090
when you start noticing how you
need to build relationships,

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:18.610
and sometimes it's not
what you're selling,

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:19.930
it's who you are.

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:23.810
The product becomes secondary
to a greater degree.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:25.670
There's a gentleman that
my father worked for

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:26.700
named A.G. Gaston,

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.900
who was one of the
first black millionaires

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:31.490
and A.G. Gaston had a mantra,

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:34.590
it was called find a need and fulfill it.

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:37.780
And so that always stuck with me.

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:40.730
And to bring you to the present,

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:43.380
comedically and where content
creation is concerned,

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:45.972
for me, it's always been about seeing,

00:03:45.972 --> 00:03:48.620
all right, what isn't out there?

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:50.650
And is there a way to make that

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:52.840
feel like something that's
already happening now?

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:55.045
I sold a project to Comedy Central

00:03:55.045 --> 00:03:57.522
that we ended up shooting a pilot for,

00:03:57.522 --> 00:03:58.500
we're still in development,

00:03:58.500 --> 00:03:59.920
and it's about a probation officer

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:01.330
and it's about a probation officer

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:03.320
and his relationship with this clients.

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:05.440
And to kind of show a different side

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:06.670
of what law enforcement is.

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:08.900
Probation is law enforcement.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.810
It's closer to social work

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:15.810
on the spectrum of kicking
ass, but it is law enforcement.

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:20.070
And we really boiled the show
down to a lot of degrees,

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:22.790
there's a procedural element to it,

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:24.890
so it feels like something
you've seen before,

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:25.900
but it's about a world

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:27.790
that you know absolutely nothing about.

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:30.960
And I think there's a
value in being unique.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:34.750
And for me, that came, I got turned,

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:36.210
I guess when I think about it now,

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:39.710
all of my best moments came
from when I was rejected.

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:44.170
BET passed on me for Comic
View for one year in 2002.

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:45.940
So much like baseball,

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:48.770
okay, y'all didn't pick
me, who did you pick?

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:49.980
Let's see what they did.

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:52.300
And I sat for an entire
season and watched Comic View.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:55.240
It's like 40 episodes,
six comics an episode,

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:56.810
and I just cataloged

00:04:56.810 --> 00:04:59.840
every topic that was breached on the show.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:01.860
And I just kept a list

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:03.130
of how many comedians talked

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:04.637
about this, this, this, this, and this.

00:05:04.637 --> 00:05:05.840
And by the end of the year,

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:08.790
I had a spreadsheet of every topic

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:11.450
that most black comics
nationally were discussing.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:13.180
And I just went okay, cool,

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:15.210
I won't do any jokes about that.

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:16.790
So let's just start there

00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:19.350
and see if we can be something

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:21.230
that hasn't been heard before it.

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:23.430
And off that alone, that
should get me a look.

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:25.780
It doesn't mean that my joke
is gonna be the funniest,

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:27.500
but it will be the most different.

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:30.340
And if I can at least have
that advantage over you

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:32.230
when it comes time to pick tapes,

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:34.380
then I think that's
something worth exploiting.

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:36.960
And so that's what I've
always tried to do.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:38.440
- It's interesting you bring that up

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:42.320
because for me, and I'm a, I talk a lot,

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:44.220
I produce so much content,

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:48.230
but so much of my strength and
so much of what I attribute

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:51.453
the good things that have
happened to me is about listening.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.090
And the story you just told

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:56.810
is a really strong listening story.

00:05:56.810 --> 00:05:59.770
The baseball story was a
strong listening story.

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:02.800
Do you feel like observation and listening

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:04.810
is foundational for you?

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:05.643
- Yeah.

00:06:05.643 --> 00:06:08.060
I think that's more
important than execution

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:10.890
because you got to know
where to aim the gun

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:11.890
before you shoot-
- It's the insight,

00:06:11.890 --> 00:06:13.520
it's the strategy.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:15.510
- Yeah, it's a sniper.

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:20.020
You're sitting for hours and
hours taking in information

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:22.982
and deciding when is
the exact proper moment

00:06:22.982 --> 00:06:24.680
to mount your attack.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.310
And so for me, it's
definitely about listening.

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:29.740
It's definitely about
assessing the marketplace,

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:33.410
seeing what's being sold
and seeing what's getting on

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:35.860
and then also trying
to pull a Wayne Gretzky

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:37.671
and be ahead of the puck,

00:06:37.671 --> 00:06:41.250
which is why COVID is so interesting to me

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:44.640
in terms of comedy creation and
content creation as a whole,

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:46.910
because not only do you have a pandemic

00:06:46.910 --> 00:06:48.020
where people are in pain

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:49.080
and people are gonna be hurting,

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:51.630
and regardless if a vaccine came tomorrow,

00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:52.780
there are gonna be a lot of people

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:54.760
that are gonna be dealing
with a lot of fiscal hurt,

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:55.860
a lot of emotional hurt.

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:57.430
I lost an aunt in February,

00:06:57.430 --> 00:07:00.070
so I can't imagine what other
people are going through.

00:07:00.070 --> 00:07:03.110
So where there's pain, there's laughter,

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:04.740
there's an opportunity for a laugh,

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:06.040
but you don't know how long.

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:06.940
And then on top of that,

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:09.200
you have an election coming
on the backside of this.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:11.020
And if you think that elections

00:07:11.020 --> 00:07:14.720
don't affect the type of content
that ends up on television,

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:16.080
you're wrong.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:17.910
So it's about looking and figuring out,

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:21.290
okay, what can you pitch or
what can you create today

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:24.930
that's going to still have an impact

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:27.680
no matter who wins in November

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:29.460
and no matter what
people are going through.

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:33.210
You know, the thing that's
interesting now about comedy,

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:34.423
Chris Rock said,

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:36.350
and I'm paraphrasing him,

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:40.560
but he basically said everything that I do

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:43.243
is to convince people to come see me live.

00:07:44.170 --> 00:07:45.500
Everything in his portfolio

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:49.050
was about getting you to
pay the $50 or whatever.

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:51.000
He's not $50, he's probably like $100 now,

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:52.613
but to come see me live.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:57.500
Come see this movie, watch
this TV show, whatever.

00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:00.240
Well, the come see me live element

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:03.660
isn't really there
right now for comedians.

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:05.790
And you don't know when
that's gonna come back.

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:08.970
So you have to do things
that maintain relevance

00:08:08.970 --> 00:08:11.070
so that when people are
ready to go back out

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:13.270
and when people have enough money

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:15.210
on a regular basis to go back out,

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:16.770
you know 'cause I'm not gonna sit here

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:17.603
and act like what I do

00:08:17.603 --> 00:08:20.360
is similar to the other people
we're gonna talk to today.

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:23.510
What I do is a disposable income activity

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:25.440
that can hopefully
alleviate a little bit of,

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:28.038
I'm no different than a sip of wine.

00:08:28.038 --> 00:08:31.580
- That's right, it's
a 100% escapism, 100%.

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:34.273
- So, you know,

00:08:34.273 --> 00:08:36.040
I think when people get back on their feet

00:08:36.040 --> 00:08:37.410
and the unemployment dries up,

00:08:37.410 --> 00:08:38.800
the first thing you aren't thinking about

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:43.180
is coming to see some jokes
for me inside of a corona hut,

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:47.590
which is what a comedy club is as far as,

00:08:47.590 --> 00:08:50.040
low ceiling, everybody's
together, that's a hut,

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:51.253
that's a corona hut.

00:08:52.190 --> 00:08:53.600
So it'll be awhile.

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:55.530
So it's about figuring out content

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:57.800
and ways to do things
that keep you relevant

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:00.456
and keep you ahead of the curve.

00:09:00.456 --> 00:09:01.289
- On that note

00:09:01.289 --> 00:09:04.300
because for everybody who's
joining in #marketingforthenow,

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:05.390
please tell us what you're thinking

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:07.200
about this interview with Roy.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:08.450
These are about 15 minute blasts.

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:09.430
So before I lose you Roy,

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:11.280
talk to me about the Call in Digital Show,

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:13.100
because I think as I'm listening to you,

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:14.120
it makes a whole lot of sense

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:16.220
why you're leaning in that direction.

00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:18.644
- Well, because I feel like,

00:09:18.644 --> 00:09:20.190
I feel like without getting
into too much detail about it,

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:23.760
I feel like it's an
opportunity to unite people.

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:26.970
And I see what's happening with
a lot of the Zoom shows now.

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:31.970
Like if you peak the game from
the beginning of quarantine

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:35.720
where you had a surge

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:37.784
in people becoming content creators,

00:09:37.784 --> 00:09:40.700
so there's fewer consumers of content.

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:42.220
Everybody's grabbing a microphone,

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:44.020
I've gone to the camera stores in New York

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:46.470
and every time I try to
get a camera component,

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:48.730
it's sold out or there's
a two week back order

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:50.980
'cause everybody's buying
the same stuff now.

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:54.460
I think that we are all one.

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:56.240
And so I did morning radio for 10 years

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:57.530
and the best part of morning radio

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:59.400
was talking to the streets.

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:02.485
And I think that's the
most interactive way

00:10:02.485 --> 00:10:04.170
to go about doing things.

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:06.600
And radio was a place where I was able

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:08.410
to really build a lot of relationships

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:09.860
because I did prank phone calls.

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:11.472
And so with the prank calls,

00:10:11.472 --> 00:10:15.420
I took prank phone calls
and this is pre YouTube,

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:17.010
this is '02, '03.

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:19.640
So the only way you went
viral was over an email

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:23.290
where if someone sent you an
email and said download this,

00:10:23.290 --> 00:10:24.600
and you would,

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:28.450
you would just download
random stuff to your laptop.

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:30.310
And that's kind of how I was able

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:32.170
to kind of grow my comedy a little bit.

00:10:32.170 --> 00:10:35.620
And so through that
what I learned was that

00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:37.070
it's laughs that connect people.

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:38.340
So if we can all laugh together,

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:41.240
then I think there's something
collective about that.

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:43.170
And so when I look at the marketplace

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:45.500
and when I find a need to fulfill,

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:46.570
I think that the sense

00:10:46.570 --> 00:10:49.253
of collective conversation comedically,

00:10:50.633 --> 00:10:53.100
most comedy conversations
are observational,

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:53.970
they're voyeuristic,

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:56.460
hey, watch me talk to this person

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:58.520
versus, hey, let's talk.

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:00.820
And I think that's the cool
thing about Instagram Live

00:11:00.820 --> 00:11:03.710
is that people have a direct line.

00:11:03.710 --> 00:11:04.543
I think there's a way

00:11:04.543 --> 00:11:08.160
to kind of set that up a
little bit more formally.

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:10.417
Real quick, my friend Carlton,

00:11:11.593 --> 00:11:14.340
what we did because I was having trouble

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:16.630
breaking into prank phone
calls in other markets,

00:11:16.630 --> 00:11:19.895
and this just goes back to
finding a need and fulfilling it,

00:11:19.895 --> 00:11:22.430
I was having trouble
breaking into comedy clubs

00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:23.263
in certain markets

00:11:23.263 --> 00:11:24.770
because I didn't have a television credit,

00:11:24.770 --> 00:11:26.520
but I peaked that most comedy clubs

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:29.720
value comedians that have media reach.

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:32.360
And so the thing I knew
about radio at the time

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:36.220
is that most radio and smaller
AA, AAA baseball cities,

00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:40.360
Omaha, Syracuse's, Columbia,
Missouri's of the world,

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:44.010
they're carrying jocks in the
afternoon or in the morning

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:45.970
that are by themselves or
it's a two person show,

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:47.630
so they don't have a lot of content.

00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:50.140
We're only playing the
prank calls in Birmingham

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:53.230
and then they'd go in a vault forever.

00:11:53.230 --> 00:11:55.000
So I would contact DJs

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:57.440
in cities where I wasn't getting
booked at the comedy club

00:11:57.440 --> 00:11:59.890
and mail them to prank
phone calls for free

00:11:59.890 --> 00:12:02.810
and just say, "Play 'em and say my name.

00:12:02.810 --> 00:12:04.260
I don't care what you
do with them after that,

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:05.670
they're yours forever."

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:06.900
And they would play them,

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:08.830
I'd let that marinate for three months,

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:10.987
then I would call the comedy club and say,

00:12:10.987 --> 00:12:13.340
"Hey, I'm on the radio
in your city every week,

00:12:13.340 --> 00:12:15.330
you should probably book me at your club."

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:18.745
And that's how I was able to
expand my reach comedically

00:12:18.745 --> 00:12:20.640
and able to leverage those fans.

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:25.420
The one mistake I made was not
having a fucking email list.

